course: everyday
lesson: 459
title: The dog that wags at last, and the one that never does
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Lily's new neighbour has a greyhound called Ghost, an ex-racing dog who never lived in a house before. When people visit, he hides behind the sofa, shaking. So every Saturday Lily sits quietly in the room with a book, never reaching out, never calling him. Week one, nothing. Week three, an eye round the sofa. Week five, Ghost walks over, puts his long nose on her knee, then leans on her with his whole weight. Lily nearly cries.
explain: Sam tries the same patience with Tank the bulldog: sitting on the wall outside number 14 every day. A year later, Tank still growls. Sam's dad says, «Not every dog's going to be your mate, son. That's alright.» You can do everything right and an animal still never comes round. That's not your failure. On Lily's cousin's farm, the guard geese haven't liked a single visitor in twenty years. «They hate the vicar most,» he says. Even the vicar can't win.
explain: Chloe's uncle in Cardiff has a rescue lurcher who took six months to let anyone but him touch her. «She's tamping if you rush her,» he says — in South Wales «tamping» means furious. On Chloe's third visit, the lurcher sniffs her shoe and gives one tiny wag. Chloe holds her breath like it's a miracle. Small signs are real wins. And Rocky, the corner-shop dog, ignored Jake for years — until Jake helped the shopkeeper carry boxes in the rain. Dogs watch who their owner trusts.
explain: Some dogs can't be trusted at all. Kieran's nan's terrier Mitzi has bitten two people, so she wears a muzzle when visitors come. «If she bites a third person, they might take her away,» Nan says. «The muzzle keeps her home with me.» Connor's dog Rex bit a toddler who pulled his ears, so Rex is never left alone with little ones again. And Kieran swears «rottweilers are killers» — until he meets Maddie's neighbour's rottweiler, owner there, and it flops on its back for a belly rub. Judge the dog, not the breed — and always ask first.
fact: Shy or frightened dogs make friends on their own terms; quiet patience works better than treats, calling or grabbing.
fact: A frightened animal's trust grows in small signs, like a sniff or a tiny wag, often over weeks or months.
fact: Some animals never warm up to a person however kindly they are treated, and that is not the person's fault.
fact: Always ask the owner before touching any dog, even one that is wagging; a wagging tail does not always mean friendly.
fact: Dogs often follow their owner's lead, so being kind and trusted by the owner can help a dog accept you.
fact: A muzzle or a short lead on a dog means «don't touch»; it keeps people safe and can let a dog that has bitten stay at home.
fact: A dog that has bitten once can bite again, so grown-ups make rules like never leaving it alone with small children.
fact: Pulling a dog's ears or tail hurts it and is a common reason dogs bite children.
fact: Each dog should be judged by how it behaves with its owner present, not just by its breed.
fact: Some farm animals, like guard geese, are not pets at all and should be left alone.
fact: In South Wales «tamping» means furious, and «cwtch» means a cuddle.
fact: Ex-racing greyhounds often have never lived in a house and need time to learn that a home is safe.
exercise: How did Lily make friends with Ghost the greyhound? | She sat quietly every week and let him come to her | She called him every week and held out a handful of treats | She pulled him out from behind the sofa | She chased him round the garden
exercise: Why was Ghost so frightened of visitors? | He was an ex-racer who had never lived in a house | He had once been bitten by Lily when he was a puppy | Greyhounds hate all people | He was too old to see properly
exercise: How long did it take before Ghost came to Lily? | About five weeks | One afternoon, at the first visit | About five whole years | He never came at all
exercise: Sam sat on the wall every day for a year. Did Tank come round? | No, he still growls | Yes, he wags at Sam now | Yes, but only for biscuits | No, because Sam didn't try hard
exercise: What did Sam's dad say about Tank? | Not every dog will be your mate, and that's alright | Keep trying every day, he'll crack and love you one day | Climb in and make him like you | Tank must be ill
exercise: «She's tamping if you rush her.» What does «tamping» mean? | Furious | Sleepy | Hungry | Excited
exercise: Why was one tiny wag from the lurcher a big deal? | Trust from a scared dog grows in small signs | It meant she'd go home with Chloe that same day | It meant she wanted to bite | Wags mean nothing at all
exercise: Why did Rocky start wagging at Jake? | Jake helped the shopkeeper Rocky trusts | Jake bought Rocky a bone | Jake started wearing a new coat that smelled of meat | Rocky forgot who Jake was
exercise: Why does Mitzi wear a muzzle when visitors come? | She has bitten two people | She likes the way it looks | Muzzles keep dogs warm | She eats too much
exercise: What does a muzzle on a dog tell you? | Don't touch | Come and pat me | I'm a puppy in training | I'm free to play
exercise: Why is Rex never left alone with little children now? | He bit a toddler who pulled his ears | He's too big for small houses and knocks them over | He doesn't like being looked at | He snores very loudly
exercise: A dog in the park is wagging and licking. What should Maddie do before stroking it? | Ask the owner first | Run up and hug it tight | Grab its collar | Feed it something
exercise: Does a wagging tail always mean a dog is friendly? | No, so you still ask first | Yes, every single time | Yes, if it wags fast | No, wagging means it's angry
exercise: Kieran said rottweilers are killers. What was the truth about this one? | It was gentle, so judge each dog, not the breed | He was right, it bit him the moment he walked near it | It was a different breed | Rottweilers can't bite
exercise: Which animals on the farm had never liked a single visitor? | The guard geese | The sheepdogs | The hens | The kittens
exercise: What's the right thing to do when an animal never comes round? | Accept it and keep your distance kindly | Keep forcing it until it gives in to you at last | Blame yourself for being horrible | Tell the owner to get rid of it
test: «bruh i've done EVERYTHING right and tank still hates me, i'm cursed». What's true? | Some dogs never warm up, and that's not your failure | Sam must be doing it wrong somehow, or Tank would like him | Tank will love him tomorrow | Dogs can smell who is cursed
test: Kieran: «muzzles are cruel, take it off her, she's sweet». What's the better view? | The muzzle keeps people safe and keeps Mitzi at home | Muzzles are always cruel, so she should be let free of it | Mitzi has never bitten anyone | Kieran should hold her mouth shut instead
test: A dog's wagging and the owner says «not this one, he's nervy». What do you do? | Leave him alone | Pat him quickly anyway | Feed him so he calms down | Stare at him until he relaxes
test: In a film, a shy rescue dog leaps into a girl's arms on day one. What's real life usually like? | It takes weeks or months, step by step | Every rescue dog jumps on you on the very first day | Rescue dogs never trust anyone | It works if the girl wears red
test: What would most likely happen if Connor's family left Rex alone with his cousin, aged three? | Rex might bite again | Rex would babysit perfectly | Rex would run away from home | Nothing, dogs forget biting
test: Lily's cousin: «them geese hate even the vicar». What's the wise thing for visitors? | Wave from the gate and stay out of their space | Bring a big bag of bread and win them over slowly | Chase them so they learn | Ask the vicar for advice
test: «the shop dog only likes jake now cos jake bribed him». What really happened? | Rocky saw his owner trusted Jake | Jake gave Rocky sausages daily | Rocky likes boys who wear hats | Jake hypnotised him
test: «rottweilers are killers, everyone knows». How should you judge a dog? | By how it behaves with its owner there | By its breed alone | By how big its head and teeth are, mostly | By what your mates say about it
test: You visit a shy dog for the first time. Which plan is best? | Sit quietly, don't reach out, let it choose | Call it loudly over and over till it comes to you | Pick it up so it can't hide | Shake a toy in its face
test: The lurcher sniffs Chloe's shoe and wags once. Her brother says «pfft, that's nothing». Who's right? | Chloe, it's a real win from a scared dog | Her brother, it means nothing | Neither, it means the dog is angry | Her brother, she should have hugged it
test: «a dog that bit once was just having a bad day, it won't happen again». True? | No, a dog that bit can bite again | Yes, dogs bite once in a lifetime | Yes, if you say sorry to it | No, it will bite every day now
exam: Identify the conduct most likely to establish trust with a fearful dog of rescue origin. | Remaining quietly present over many weeks without reaching towards it | Offering treats loudly and repeatedly at every available opportunity | Extracting the dog from its hiding place | Pursuing the dog until it ceases to flee
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of a muzzle upon a dog? | Persons should not touch the dog, and it may have bitten before | The dog is particularly eager to be stroked | The muzzle serves chiefly to keep the dog warm | The dog is a puppy under training
exam: Given that a dog has previously bitten a small child, ascertain the most appropriate household rule. | The dog shall never be left alone with small children | The dog shall be permitted all contact as before | The child shall be instructed to pull its ears gently | No rule is required since dogs bite but once
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning an animal that fails to accept a person despite patient and kind conduct. | Such outcomes occur and do not signify failure on that person's part | They prove that the person has secretly behaved cruelly towards the animal | They are impossible if treats are offered | They are remedied by increased force
